def escape_cell(text):
    """A literal | inside a cell would end the cell, so escape it; newlines would end the row."""
    return str(text).replace('|', '\\|').replace('\n', ' ')


def two_column_table(pairs, headers=('Name', 'Value'), align=('left', 'left')):
    marks = {'left': ':---', 'right': '---:', 'center': ':---:'}
    lines = [f'| {escape_cell(headers[0])} | {escape_cell(headers[1])} |',
             f'| {marks[align[0]]} | {marks[align[1]]} |']
    lines += [f'| {escape_cell(a)} | {escape_cell(b)} |' for a, b in pairs]
    return '\n'.join(lines)
```

A two-column Markdown table is a header row, a separator row (the colons set the alignment) and one row per pair, each on its own line. Two details break tables in practice: a `|` inside a cell (it must be escaped as `\|`) and a line break inside a cell (a row must stay on one line). The helper handles both, so the table always renders.

